# Make an arcane spellcaster and get into the dungeon.
#
# The character is a male orc mage, neutral, specialised in Evocation, and
# worshipping whichever god the game offers. The race is the same orc the
# other chargen scripts use, on purpose: it keeps the race and subrace letters
# identical across the family of scripts, so only the class-specific part of
# this file differs from chargen.keys and only that part can rot.
#
# Why a mage at all: a barbarian never touches the spell system. A mage
# reaches spell selection during generation, then the casting code, the mana
# code and the targeting code during play, which is a large body of the
# ruleset that no melee script exercises.
#
# Evocation is chosen over the other eight schools because evocation spells
# are the ones that take a target and do damage. A specialist in, say,
# Divination would cast spells that end without touching combat resolution.
#
# Every choice here is a menu letter, and menu letters move when the list
# changes. Run with a fixed seed (tools/headless.sh <script> <seed>) or the
# attribute rolls change, the offered feats change with them, and these
# letters choose something else.

a       # Initial Choices: [a] Create a New Character
n       # Alter the character generation options? -- no
y       # Your options will place the game in Explore Mode. Continue? -- yes
i       # Race:     [i] Orc
a       # Subrace:  [a] Orc (Standard)
d       # Class:    [d] Mage
a       # Attributes: the first of the five offered sets
e       # Alignment: [e] Neutral
        #   NOTE: this is not the [c] that chargen.keys uses. A barbarian may
        #   not be lawful, so the game offers a barbarian a shortened list of
        #   six alignments and Neutral falls on [c]. A mage may be anything,
        #   so the list is the full nine and Neutral falls on [e].
e       # Wizard specialty: [e] Evocation
a       # First feat
a       # Second feat

# Skill Manager. RIGHT spends a rank on the selected skill and stops at the
# skill's maximum, DOWN moves to the next skill, so three RIGHTs per skill
# spends everything available without needing to know how many ranks this
# character has. Character generation will not let go until the pool is empty.
RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN
RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN
ENTER y # Done, and confirm keeping the ranks

y       # Do you wish to worship <god>? -- yes
        #   Unlike a priest, a mage gains no second pool of skill ranks from
        #   the choice of god, so no second Skill Manager pass follows here.

a       # Personality archetype

# Three starting spells. A first-level mage is offered exactly three picks on
# every seed tested; the menu says how many are left, and the run ends on the
# room description rather than on a spell list when the count is right.
a a a

# The next keystroke dismisses the arrival room description and lands in the
# dungeon, in play mode.
